From 554d8af0fa08075141c5567c65c89f0369cdc3ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakr233 <git@qaq.wiki>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 10:45:53 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Write a whole imageblock region when the slice write names
 one

An explicit-region air.write_imageblock_slice_to_texture writes every
cell of the region. macOS 27's CoreAnimation blur kernels depend on it:
the threads fill a shared imageblock tile, meet at a barrier, and one
thread writes the whole tile with region 32x32 or 16x32. The per-cell
lowering stored only the calling thread's cell, so each tile produced
one texel and Liquid Glass sampled an empty backdrop.

- A slice write with the explicit-region flag set and a region that is
  not constant-empty becomes a call to a helper function that walks the
  region with two loops, reads each cell from the shared array and
  writes it, clipped to the image. Zero-area constant regions keep the
  existing transparent-texel behaviour.
- The destination is bound at one level and OpImageWrite takes none, so
  writes that name a level other than 0 are dropped instead of landing
  on level 0.
- A compute kernel with such a write and no APV extent gets a 32x32
  shared tile. The emitter used a per-invocation private scratch when
  the kernel had no threads_per_threadgroup parameter, which hid every
  other thread's cells from the writing thread. Any row stride at least
  as wide as the imageblock keeps cells apart.

Verified: cargo test --release --lib, 1331 pass including the new
native_kernel_imageblock_region_write_walks_the_whole_region, which
runs spirv-val. clippy has no new warnings. On arm64 Linux (NVIDIA
GB10) with Reims at protocol 237, a 27.2 guest's blur chain went from
all-zero output to the expected backdrop, and its About window drew as
frosted glass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
